 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Infinite Speed Range:** Speed has an infinite value range, so a linear map is not feasible. A logarithmic sampling is used to handle this [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Shallow vs. Deep:** Shallow neighborhood estimation using $\nabla v \cdot \vec{r}$ reflects only the immediate vicinity. Deep neighborhood estimation reveals internal structures but requires iterative search [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Infinite Speed Range:** Speed has an infinite value range, so a linear map is not feasible [S1, unofficial]. A logarithmic sampling is used to handle the infinite range [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Shallow vs. Deep:** Shallow neighborhood estimation using $\nabla v \cdot \vec{r}$ reflects only the immediate vicinity [S1, unofficial]. Deep neighborhood estimation reveals internal structures but requires iterative search [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Shallow Neighborhood Estimation
-Estimates the scalar changing rate using the directional derivative $\nabla v \cdot \vec{r}$ to reflect the immediate vicinity of the isosurface [S1, unofficial]. Sets itself apart from deep estimation by avoiding iterative searches, making it faster but less effective at revealing internal structures [S1, unofficial]. When to choose it: Not given in the sources. Link: [Shallow Neighborhood Estimation](shallow-neighborhood-estimation.md).
+Estimates the scalar changing rate using the directional derivative $\nabla v \cdot \vec{r}$ to reflect the immediate vicinity of the isosurface [S1, unofficial]. Sets itself apart from deep estimation by avoiding iterative searches, making it faster but less effective at revealing internal structures [S1, unofficial]. Link: [Shallow Neighborhood Estimation](shallow-neighborhood-estimation.md).
 
 ### Deep Neighborhood Estimation
-Estimates the scalar changing rate by performing an iterative search along the viewing ray to find $\Delta l$ where the scalar reaches $l_{iso} + \Delta v$, then computing $\Delta l / \Delta v$ [S1, unofficial]. Sets itself apart from shallow estimation by revealing internal structures behind the isosurface at the cost of additional computation [S1, unofficial]. When to choose it: Not given in the sources. Link: [Deep Neighborhood Estimation](deep-neighborhood-estimation.md).
+Estimates the scalar changing rate by performing an iterative search along the viewing ray to find $\Delta l$ where the scalar reaches $l_{iso} + \Delta v$, then computing $\Delta l / \Delta v$ [S1, unofficial]. Sets itself apart from shallow estimation by revealing internal structures behind the isosurface at the cost of additional computation [S1, unofficial]. Link: [Deep Neighborhood Estimation](deep-neighborhood-estimation.md).
 
